---
export const prerender = false;
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FaqAccordion from "../../../components/FaqAccordion.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "What's the difference between a generative AI course and an agentic AI course?",
    answer: "A generative AI course teaches you to work with a tool one exchange at a time: you ask, it answers, you check and adjust. An agentic AI course covers systems that carry out multi-step tasks on their own, pulling data, applying rules, and taking action with limited supervision. Most people should learn generative AI first. See our Agentic AI Course page for the next step once you've got the basics down.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need to know how to code to learn generative AI?",
    answer: "No. The skills that matter most for most roles, writing clear prompts, checking output for accuracy, and fitting AI into an existing workflow, don't require programming. Coding becomes relevant only if you want to connect AI tools to other software through an API, which is a separate and more advanced skill most learners don't need at the start.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is there a good free generative AI course?",
    answer: "Free tiers of tools like ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini let you learn the basics without paying anything, and plenty of free tutorials exist online. What free resources usually don't do is connect what you're learning to your specific job, industry, or company's tools. Free is a fine starting point. It's rarely enough on its own if you need to apply this at work with any consistency.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long does it take to learn generative AI?",
    answer: "Getting comfortable with the basics, prompt structure, iteration, and common use cases, usually takes a few focused hours spread over a week or two of regular practice. Getting genuinely good at applying it to your specific job takes longer and comes mostly from repetition on real tasks, not from watching more tutorials.",
  },
  {
    question: "What's the difference between ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini for learning purposes?",
    answer: "For learning fundamentals, the differences matter less than people expect. All three handle prompting, drafting, and summarizing well enough to build core skills. Once you're comfortable with the basics, differences in reasoning style, context length, and integration options start to matter more, and which tool fits best often comes down to what your company or industry has already standardized on.",
  },
];

const faqLdJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.answer },
  })),
};

const courseLdJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Course',
  name: "Generative AI Course",
  description: "A practical generative AI course covering how large language models work, prompt structure, and applied use cases for writing, research, and everyday work tasks.",
  provider: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Lenoo AI', sameAs: 'https://lenooai.com' },
  url: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-courses/generative-ai',
};

const breadcrumbLdJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://lenooai.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Services', item: 'https://lenooai.com/services' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'AI Courses', item: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-courses' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 4, name: 'Generative AI Course', item: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-courses/generative-ai' },
  ],
};

Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', 's-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=300');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Generative AI Course: What to Learn and Where to Start | Lenoo AI"
  description="A practical generative AI course guide: what to actually learn, free vs paid options, and how to build real skill with ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini for your job."
  canonicalPath="/services/ai-courses/generative-ai"
  ldJson={[faqLdJson, courseLdJson, breadcrumbLdJson]}
>

<!-- ── HERO ── -->
<header class="hero-section" style="padding-top: 10rem; padding-bottom: 5rem; text-align: center;">
  <div class="container" style="position: relative; z-index: 1;">
    <div style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;background:rgba(0,227,253,0.12);border:1px solid rgba(0,227,253,0.28);border-radius:100px;padding:6px 16px;color:#a8f2ff;font-size:0.875rem;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1.5rem;" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">Home</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span><a href="/services" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">Services</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span><a href="/services/ai-courses" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">AI Courses</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span>Generative AI Course</div>
    <h1 style="max-width: 46rem; margin: 0 auto 1.5rem;">
      Generative AI <span class="gradient-text">Course</span>
    </h1>
    <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.72); font-size: 1.125rem; line-height: 1.75; max-width: 40rem; margin: 0 auto 0;">
      Most people who say they want to "learn AI" actually mean generative AI: tools like ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini that write, summarize, and answer questions on request. This page covers what a real generative AI course should teach you, in what order, and what you can reasonably learn for free before it's worth investing in something more structured.
    </p>
    <div class="trust-chips" style="justify-content:center; margin:1.75rem 0 2rem;">
      <span class="tchip">No coding required</span>
      <span class="tchip">Works across ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini</span>
      <span class="tchip">Foundational path before agentic AI</span>
    </div>
    <div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem; justify-content: center;">
      <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="courses_generative_ai_hero" class="btn-primary" style="font-size: 1rem;">Request Training Quote →</button>
      <a href="#curriculum" class="btn-outline" style="font-size: 1rem;">See the Curriculum ↓</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ── WHY THIS MATTERS ── -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 800px;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
      <h2 id="why-heading">Why Start With Generative AI</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Generative AI is the foundation everything else builds on. Before you can usefully automate a workflow or build an AI agent, you need a working sense of how these models respond to instructions, where they're reliable, and where they confidently get things wrong. Skipping this step and jumping straight to more advanced tools usually means relearning the same lessons later, under more pressure.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      It's also the part of AI that applies most directly to daily work, regardless of role. Writing a first draft, summarizing a long document, rephrasing something for a different audience, checking your own writing for gaps: these are tasks nearly every job includes, and generative AI handles them well once you know how to ask properly.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      The gap between people who get real value from these tools and people who find them frustrating usually isn't the tool. It's whether they've learned to give clear instructions, provide the right context, and check the output critically instead of accepting the first answer.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      There's a search-behavior pattern worth naming directly: a lot of people looking for a "generative AI course" are really looking for permission to slow down and learn this properly, instead of picking things up piecemeal from coworkers or social media. That's a reasonable instinct. Piecemeal learning tends to produce bad habits that are harder to unlearn later, like phrasing every request the same generic way regardless of the task, or trusting output without checking it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── CURRICULUM ── -->
<section id="curriculum" class="section" style="background: #e7eeff;" aria-labelledby="curriculum-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
      <p style="color: #006875; font-size: .8rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; margin-bottom: 1rem;">What You'll Learn</p>
      <h2 id="curriculum-heading">Core Curriculum</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="path-grid">
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">How These Models Actually Work</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">A plain-English mental model, not the underlying math: what a large language model is predicting, why it sometimes states wrong things confidently, and why the same prompt can produce slightly different answers each time. This context makes every later lesson make more sense.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Prompt Structure Fundamentals</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">The difference between a vague request and one that gets a usable answer on the first try: giving the model a role, the relevant context, clear constraints, and the format you actually need the output in.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Getting Consistent, Checkable Output</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">How to iterate on a bad answer instead of starting over, how to ask a model to show its reasoning, and simple habits for verifying facts and figures before you use them anywhere that matters.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Writing and Drafting</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">Using AI for first drafts of emails, reports, and documents, then editing rather than accepting output wholesale. The goal is a faster starting point, not outsourcing your judgment on the final version.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Research and Analysis, With Caution</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">Using AI to summarize long documents and pull out key points, alongside an honest look at hallucination risk: why models can invent plausible-sounding facts, and how to catch it before it costs you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card path-card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.75rem; color:#00253b;">Image and Video Generation</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;">A shorter overview of visual generation tools: what they're currently good at, where they still fall short, and how prompting for images differs from prompting for text.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── FORMAT & PATH ── -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="format-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 760px;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2 id="format-heading">Who This Course Is For</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      If you've never used ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini for more than a casual question, start here. This curriculum assumes no prior experience and builds the fundamentals that every other AI skill, including agentic AI and prompt engineering, depends on. If you're starting from absolute zero, our <a href="/services/ai-courses/beginners" style="color:#006875; font-weight:600;">AI Courses for Beginners</a> page is an even gentler entry point.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      If you've already used these tools casually but feel like you're not getting consistent results, this is also for you. Most people in that position have skipped the prompt structure fundamentals and gone straight to trial and error, which works sometimes and wastes time the rest of the time.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      This is not a course for people who already build software on top of AI models through code, or who are looking for machine learning theory. If that's you, the terminology and pace here will feel too introductory. This page is built for the much larger group of professionals who need to use these tools well inside their existing job, not build new products with them.
    </p>
    <div style="text-align:center;margin-top:2.5rem;padding:2rem;background:#f9f9ff;border-radius:20px;max-width:640px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;">
      <p style="color:#00253b;font-weight:700;font-size:1.0625rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Covered by our 100% refund guarantee</p>
      <p style="color:#42474e;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Want your whole team trained together on this?</p>
      <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="courses_generative_ai_mid" class="btn-primary">Request Training Quote →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── FREE VS PAID LANDSCAPE ── -->
<section class="section" style="background: #f0f3ff;" aria-labelledby="landscape-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 800px;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2 id="landscape-heading">Free vs. Structured: What Each Actually Gets You</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Free resources cover this material reasonably well. YouTube tutorials, vendor documentation from OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google, and the free tiers of the tools themselves are enough for a motivated individual to reach a working baseline. That's a genuine option if you're learning on your own time with no deadline.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      What free content generally doesn't do is connect this to your actual job. A general tutorial can teach you to summarize a document. It can't teach you to summarize the specific kind of report your company produces, using the terminology your industry expects, in a way your manager will accept without heavy editing. That gap is where a lot of self-taught learners plateau.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      If you're learning for yourself, free is a fine place to start and this page gives you the order to learn things in. If your company wants a whole team brought up to speed together, with content built around your actual tools and workflows, that's a different exercise, and it's what our <a href="/services/ai-training" style="color:#006875; font-weight:600;">AI training and workshops</a> service is built for.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      One more honest note on paid course platforms specifically: a certificate from a generic online course rarely changes anything on its own. What employers and clients actually notice is whether you can produce a usable result on a real task, quickly and without hand-holding. Optimize for that skill, not for a badge to add to a profile.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── WHERE TO GO NEXT ── -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="next-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 760px;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2 id="next-heading">Where to Go Next</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Once the fundamentals above feel comfortable, most learners branch in one of two directions. If you want to get sharper at getting exactly the output you want on the first try, our <a href="/services/ai-courses/prompt-engineering" style="color:#006875; font-weight:600;">Prompt Engineering Course</a> goes deeper into structuring instructions for consistent results.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      If you're more interested in systems that act on their own, pulling data, applying rules, and completing multi-step tasks without you prompting each step, our <a href="/services/ai-courses/agentic-ai" style="color:#006875; font-weight:600;">Agentic AI Course</a> is the next step up. It assumes you're already comfortable with what's covered on this page.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      Either direction is a reasonable next move. There's no wrong order beyond making sure the fundamentals above feel solid first, since both paths build directly on the habits you develop here.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── FAQ ── -->
<section class="section" style="background: #e7eeff;" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 48rem;">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
      <h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    </div>
    <FaqAccordion items={faqItems} />
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── FINAL CTA ── -->
<section class="section-sm" aria-label="Call to action">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="cta-band">
      <div style="position: relative; z-index: 1; text-align:center;">
        <p class="cta-eyebrow">Free Discovery Call</p>
        <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.5rem); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Want Your Whole Team Trained Together?</h2>
        <p style="font-size: 1.125rem; max-width: 36rem; margin: 0 auto 2rem; line-height: 1.7;">Self-study works for one person. If you need a team aligned on the same tools and habits, request a quote for private generative AI training built around your business.</p>
        <div class="cta-trust-badges">
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ 100% free</span>
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ No commitment</span>
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ Refund guarantee</span>
        </div>
        <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="courses_generative_ai" class="btn-primary" style="font-size: 1.125rem; padding: 1.125rem 2.75rem;">Request Training Quote →</button>
        <p style="font-size: .875rem; margin-top: 1rem; color:#94a3b8;">30-min call · No sales pressure</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .path-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .path-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
